// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  POST /schools/current/grading-schemes
// Needs X-School-Subdomain. NO academic year in the path.
//
//  A RULEBOOK OUTLIVES A YEAR. The same scheme grades 2026-2027 and
//  2027-2028, and a report card from either must reprint identically years
//  later. schemeVersion moves when the RULES move, not the calendar.
//
//  WHICH IS WHY ONLY TWO GATES RUN. Gate 4 asks whether a named academic year
//  is the school's working one, and there is no year here to ask it about. So
//  this is the one academics surface that still answers after the year has
//  been ended - and it has to, because correcting a 2026 report card means
//  reading the 2026 scheme.
//
//  THE SCALE IS READ FIRST. PERCENTAGE and MARKS require maximumValue and
//  bounded bands; DESCRIPTOR refuses both. Asking about a band before knowing
//  the scale gives the right refusal for the wrong reason.
//
//  IT NAMES WHAT A TEACHER ENTERS, never what comes out. gradeCode is the
//  awarded grade and gradePoint is what it is worth - both mapped FROM a value
//  on one of these scales. POINT was renamed MARKS on 2026-09-14 because it
//  read as a grade point, and a scheme keyed on grade points would be mapping
//  grade points onto grade points.
//
//  AN OVERLAP IS REFUSED; A GAP IS ONLY REPORTED. A gap means one mark has no
//  grade - visible and fixable. An overlap means one mark has TWO, and which
//  wins depends on the order the bands happen to be stored in.
//
//  BOUNDS ARE INCLUSIVE AT BOTH ENDS, so two bands must not touch: 81-90
//  beside 90-100 both claim 90.
//
//  WHICH MEANS A SCALE CAN NEVER BE TILED. 81-90 beside 91-100 misses 90.5,
//  and closing that is an overlap. So gaps are counted in WHOLE MARKS:
//  90 -> 91 is silent, 32 -> 81 is not. The cost is that a school awarding
//  90.5 is not warned; #8 will answer 404 for it.
//
//  name + schemeVersion IS THE KEY, and there is no PATCH at all. Every field
//  is either half the key, a reinterpretation of every band beneath it
//  (scaleType, maximumValue), the history itself (gradeBands), or an event
//  with its own endpoints (active).
// =========================================================================
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 01  THE CBSE SCALE                                     -> 201 Created
//     The body above - eight bands, covering 0 to 100.
//     OUT: gradingSchemeDocsId, bandCount 8, active true, and NO warning.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 02  AN IB SCHEME - 1 TO 7 AS THE AWARDED GRADE         -> 201 Created
//     IB's 1-7 is the grade AWARDED, not the number a teacher enters. So the
//     codes are "7" down to "1", the scale is PERCENTAGE, a raw score resolves
//     the band, and gradePoint carries the 1-7 for aggregation.
//     THE BOUNDARIES MOVE EVERY SESSION - IB sets them per subject after the
//     papers are marked, so a 7 is roughly 75-85% and a raw 72% can be a 7 one
//     session and a 6 the next. Each session is a new schemeVersion.
//
//     { "name": "IB Diploma HL - May 2026 boundaries", "schemeVersion": "2026.5",
//       "scaleType": "PERCENTAGE", "maximumValue": 100,
//       "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "7", "minimumValue": 84, "maximumValue": 100, "gradePoint": 7 },
//         { "gradeCode": "6", "minimumValue": 72, "maximumValue": 83, "gradePoint": 6 },
//         { "gradeCode": "5", "minimumValue": 59, "maximumValue": 71, "gradePoint": 5 },
//         { "gradeCode": "4", "minimumValue": 46, "maximumValue": 58, "gradePoint": 4 },
//         { "gradeCode": "1", "minimumValue": 0, "maximumValue": 45, "gradePoint": 1, "passed": false } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 02b A PAPER MARKED OUT OF 50                           -> 201 Created
//     What MARKS is for: a raw total that is not 100. The same walk as
//     PERCENTAGE - the difference is the REPORTED figure, since "43 / 50" is
//     not "86%", which is why ReportCardSubjectResult carries maximumMarks and
//     percentage as two fields.
//
//     { "name": "Unit Test - out of 50", "schemeVersion": "2026.1",
//       "scaleType": "MARKS", "maximumValue": 50,
//       "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "A", "minimumValue": 40, "maximumValue": 50, "gradePoint": 10 },
//         { "gradeCode": "B", "minimumValue": 30, "maximumValue": 39, "gradePoint": 8 },
//         { "gradeCode": "C", "minimumValue": 17, "maximumValue": 29, "gradePoint": 6 },
//         { "gradeCode": "D", "minimumValue": 0, "maximumValue": 16, "passed": false } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 03  A DESCRIPTOR SCALE, WITH NO NUMBERS AT ALL         -> 201 Created
//     No maximumValue and no bounds. This was impossible to store until
//     2026-09-13, when GradeBand's bounds stopped being @NotNull.
//     OUT: no warning - there is no scale to leave holes in.
//
//     { "name": "Early Years Descriptors", "schemeVersion": "2026.1",
//       "scaleType": "DESCRIPTOR",
//       "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "SECURE", "description": "Secure" },
//         { "gradeCode": "DEVELOPING", "description": "Developing" },
//         { "gradeCode": "BEGINNING", "description": "Beginning", "passed": false } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 04  A DESCRIPTOR SCHEME CARRYING A CEILING         -> 400 Bad Request
//     Send case 03 with "maximumValue": 100 added.
//     OUT: { "code": "GRADE_BAND_BOUNDS_NOT_ALLOWED" }. The scale is checked
//     BEFORE any band, so this is what you hear about first.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 05  TWO BANDS THAT TOUCH                               -> 409 Conflict
//     OUT: { "code": "GRADE_BANDS_OVERLAP" }, naming both and the value they
//     share. Bounds are inclusive at BOTH ends, so 90 belongs to each -
//     unlike term dates, where the 30th and the 1st share no day.
//
//     { "name": "Touching", "schemeVersion": "1", "scaleType": "PERCENTAGE",
//       "maximumValue": 100, "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "A2", "minimumValue": 81, "maximumValue": 90 },
//         { "gradeCode": "A1", "minimumValue": 90, "maximumValue": 100 } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 06  A HOLE A WHOLE MARK FITS IN                        -> 201 Created
//     ACCEPTED, with a warning naming "32 to 81". Marks 33 to 80 resolve to
//     nothing. Reported rather than refused: a gap a school left on purpose
//     is indistinguishable from one it did not mean.
//
//     { "name": "Gappy", "schemeVersion": "1", "scaleType": "PERCENTAGE",
//       "maximumValue": 100, "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "E", "minimumValue": 0, "maximumValue": 32, "passed": false },
//         { "gradeCode": "A2", "minimumValue": 81, "maximumValue": 90 },
//         { "gradeCode": "A1", "minimumValue": 91, "maximumValue": 100 } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 07  AN UNGRADED CEILING                                -> 201 Created
//     ACCEPTED, with a warning naming "99 to 100". The trailing edge is
//     CLOSED at the ceiling - 100 itself has no grade, and no other edge
//     would notice. One mark, and still worth saying.
//
//     { "name": "Short", "schemeVersion": "1", "scaleType": "PERCENTAGE",
//       "maximumValue": 100, "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "ALL", "minimumValue": 0, "maximumValue": 99 } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 08  A BAND PAST THE CEILING                            -> 409 Conflict
//     OUT: { "code": "GRADE_BAND_OUTSIDE_SCALE" }. A grade no mark could ever
//     reach is a row a school would stare at without working out why it never
//     appears.
//
//     { "name": "Overshoot", "schemeVersion": "1", "scaleType": "MARKS",
//       "maximumValue": 7, "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "EIGHT", "minimumValue": 7.5, "maximumValue": 8 } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 09  THE SAME NAME AND VERSION AGAIN                    -> 409 Conflict
//     Send case 01 twice. OUT: { "code": "SCHEME_VERSION_TAKEN" }. The PAIR
//     is the key - the same name at a different version is fine, and is what
//     #2 exists to create.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 VALIDATION_FAILED                A missing name, schemeVersion, scaleType or gradeBands; an empty band list; over 40 bands.
//     400 SCALE_MAXIMUM_REQUIRED           A PERCENTAGE/MARKS scheme with no maximumValue, or one at or below zero.
//     400 GRADE_BAND_BOUNDS_REQUIRED       A PERCENTAGE/MARKS band missing a bound. One bound alone is not a range.
//     400 GRADE_BAND_BOUNDS_NOT_ALLOWED    A DESCRIPTOR band carrying bounds, or a DESCRIPTOR scheme carrying maximumValue.
//     400 INVALID_GRADE_BAND_RANGE         One band's maximumValue is below its minimumValue. Equal bounds are legal.
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED              The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND                 No school has that subdomain.
//     409 GRADE_BAND_CODE_TAKEN            Two bands share a gradeCode, case-insensitively.
//     409 GRADE_BAND_OUTSIDE_SCALE         A band reaches past maximumValue or below zero.
//     409 GRADE_BANDS_OVERLAP              Two bands cover the same value. Touching counts.
//     409 SCHEME_VERSION_TAKEN             This school already has that name at that version.
//     409 SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE                Gate 1.
//     409 SUBSCRIPTION_NOT_USABLE          Gate 2.

{
  "name": "CBSE Percentage Grading (corrected)"
}

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  PATCH /schools/current/grading-schemes/{id}
// Needs X-School-Subdomain. NO academic year. Every field is optional.
//
//  EVERY FIELD IS EDITABLE - while NOTHING references the scheme. The moment
//  a subject, exam or report card points at it, only `active` is and the
//  rest is 409 SCHEME_STILL_REFERENCED.
//
//  THE PLAN SAID THERE WOULD BE NO PATCH AT ALL. It reasoned field by field:
//  the key cannot move, the scale reinterprets every band, the bands are the
//  history. Each is true of a scheme something has USED and none is true of
//  one nothing has - so the rule moved from the FIELD to the STATE.
//
//  active IS THE EXCEPTION because it is the one field that does not change
//  what a printed grade means. Retiring a scheme everything uses is exactly
//  what a school does when publishing the next version. (#4 and #5 were
//  dropped for this reason - they only ever set this field.)
//
//  EVERY RULE #1 APPLIES IS RE-APPLIED TO THE RESULTING SCHEME, not the
//  body. That is what makes a half-change safe to send.
//
//  maximumValue IS DERIVED ON A DESCRIPTOR SCALE - the one place absent does
//  not mean "leave it alone". A PATCH cannot say "remove this number", so
//  keeping the stored ceiling would make PERCENTAGE -> DESCRIPTOR
//  impossible. There is exactly one legal value: absent.
//
//  THE BAND SET IS REPLACED WHOLE OR NOT AT ALL. An empty list is a 400.
//
//  NOTHING IS WRITTEN until every check passes.
// =========================================================================
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 01  A RENAME, AND NOTHING ELSE                          -> 200 OK
//     The body above. Bands, scale and ceiling all untouched.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 02  AN EMPTY BODY                                   -> 400 Bad Request
//     OUT: { "code": "NOTHING_TO_UPDATE" }, checked before anything is read.
//
//     { }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 03  LOWER THE CEILING UNDER A BAND YOU DID NOT SEND    -> 409 Conflict
//     On the CBSE scale. OUT: { "code": "GRADE_BAND_OUTSIDE_SCALE" } - A1
//     still reaches 100. THE POINT OF CHECKING THE RESULTING SCHEME.
//
//     { "maximumValue": 50 }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 04  SWITCH TO DESCRIPTOR WITHOUT NEW BANDS         -> 400 Bad Request
//     OUT: { "code": "GRADE_BAND_BOUNDS_NOT_ALLOWED" }. The stored bands
//     still carry bounds. Scale and bands are ONE change.
//
//     { "scaleType": "DESCRIPTOR" }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 05  SWITCH TO DESCRIPTOR, PROPERLY                      -> 200 OK
//     OUT: scaleType DESCRIPTOR and maximumValue GONE - derived, not sent.
//
//     { "scaleType": "DESCRIPTOR", "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "SECURE", "description": "Secure" },
//         { "gradeCode": "DEVELOPING", "description": "Developing" },
//         { "gradeCode": "BEGINNING", "passed": false } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 06  AND BACK AGAIN, WITHOUT A CEILING              -> 400 Bad Request
//     OUT: { "code": "SCALE_MAXIMUM_REQUIRED" }, which says what to send -
//     which is why the reverse switch needs no derivation rule.
//
//     { "scaleType": "PERCENTAGE", "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "A", "minimumValue": 0, "maximumValue": 100 } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 07  REPLACE THE WHOLE BAND SET                          -> 200 OK
//     Whole or not at all - there is no per-band edit.
//
//     { "gradeBands": [
//         { "gradeCode": "P", "minimumValue": 33, "maximumValue": 100 },
//         { "gradeCode": "F", "minimumValue": 0, "maximumValue": 32, "passed": false } ] }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 08  ONCE A SUBJECT USES IT                             -> 409 Conflict
//     Attach it with Add Subject, then send ANY case above. OUT:
//     { "code": "SCHEME_STILL_REFERENCED" }, naming the scheme and saying
//     which collections were actually checked.
//     THIS IS THE ENDPOINT'S WHOLE GUARD.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 09  EXCEPT active, WHICH STILL WORKS                    -> 200 OK
//     Same referenced scheme. Send true to put it back.
//
//     { "active": false }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 NOTHING_TO_UPDATE                A body that asks for nothing.
//     400 SCHEME_KEY_REQUIRED              "name": "" or "schemeVersion": "" - replaced, never removed.
//     400 GRADE_BANDS_REQUIRED             An empty band list. Send active false to retire instead.
//     400 SCALE_MAXIMUM_REQUIRED           Switching away from DESCRIPTOR with no ceiling.
//     400 GRADE_BAND_BOUNDS_REQUIRED       A PERCENTAGE/MARKS band missing a bound.
//     400 GRADE_BAND_BOUNDS_NOT_ALLOWED    DESCRIPTOR while the stored bands still carry bounds.
//     400 INVALID_GRADE_BAND_RANGE         A band running backwards.
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED              The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND                 No school has that subdomain.
//     404 GRADING_SCHEME_NOT_FOUND         No scheme with that id in this school.
//     409 SCHEME_STILL_REFERENCED          A subject grades by it. Only active is still editable.
//     409 SCHEME_VERSION_TAKEN             The new name + version pair is already taken.
//     409 GRADE_BAND_CODE_TAKEN            Two bands share a gradeCode.
//     409 GRADE_BAND_OUTSIDE_SCALE         A band reaches past the ceiling.
//     409 GRADE_BANDS_OVERLAP              Two bands cover the same value.
//     409 SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE                Gate 1.
//     409 SUBSCRIPTION_NOT_USABLE          Gate 2.

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  GET /schools/current/grading-schemes/{id}/resolve?value=
// A GET — no body. Needs X-School-Subdomain and ?value=.
//
//  THIS IS WHAT THE WHOLE MODULE IS FOR. Every rule #1 enforces on WRITE was
//  unexercised on READ until this existed — a band set that VALIDATES but
//  RESOLVES wrongly is the failure mode a create-only module cannot detect.
//
//  IT WRITES NOTHING. Arithmetic over one document, which is why the value is
//  in the query string. A resolved grade is stored by whatever records the
//  mark, not by the act of asking what it is.
//
//  THREE REFUSALS, AND THE ORDER IS THE ANSWER
//     1. 409 SCHEME_NOT_RESOLVABLE_BY_VALUE   DESCRIPTOR has no arithmetic to do
//     2. 400 VALUE_OUTSIDE_SCALE              a mark this scheme could not produce
//     3. 404 GRADE_NOT_RESOLVABLE             the scale has a hole there
//  Any other order gives a true answer to the wrong question: a descriptor
//  scheme asked about 90 would be told "outside the scale" when it has none.
//
//  A GAP IS A 404, NOT A 409. The caller asked for the grade at this value and
//  there is none. The message NAMES the two bands it fell between, because the
//  school is the only one who can close the hole.
//
//  BOTH BOUNDS ARE INCLUSIVE, which is unambiguous ONLY because an overlap is
//  refused at write. The two rules are halves of one decision.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  A MARK RESOLVES                   -> 200  the WHOLE band: gradeCode,
//                                              gradePoint, description, passed,
//                                              and the band's own bounds.
// 02  BOTH BOUNDS ARE INCLUSIVE         -> 200  A1 is 91–100: 91 and 100 both
//                                              resolve to A1, 90 to A2.
// 03  A HOLE IN THE SCALE               -> 404  GRADE_NOT_RESOLVABLE, naming the
//                                              band below and the band above.
// 04  ABOVE THE CEILING                 -> 400  VALUE_OUTSIDE_SCALE. Checked
//                                              BEFORE the bands.
// 05  BELOW ZERO                        -> 400  same.
// 06  A DESCRIPTOR SCHEME               -> 409  SCHEME_NOT_RESOLVABLE_BY_VALUE,
//                                              even for ?value=9999.
// 07  A MARKS SCHEME OUT OF 50          -> 200  50 resolves, 51 is outside.
// 08  A FRACTIONAL MARK IN A SLIVER     -> 404  19.5 between 0–19 and 20–50.
//                                              The cost #1's gap warning names:
//                                              it measures in WHOLE marks.
// 09  A RETIRED SCHEME STILL RESOLVES   -> 200  active governs what is OFFERED.
// 10  NO ?value= AT ALL                 -> 400  and ?value=abc is a 400 too.
// 11  ANOTHER SCHOOL'S SCHEME           -> 404  GRADING_SCHEME_NOT_FOUND.
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED              The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 VALUE_OUTSIDE_SCALE              ?value= is below 0 or above maximumValue.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND                 No school has that subdomain.
//     404 GRADING_SCHEME_NOT_FOUND         Not a scheme of this school.
//     404 GRADE_NOT_RESOLVABLE             No band covers the value.
//     409 SCHEME_NOT_RESOLVABLE_BY_VALUE   The scheme is DESCRIPTOR.

request.description:
// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  POST /schools/current/timetables
//
//  A RANGE, BECAUSE A SCHOOL BUILDS A PATTERN AND NOT A TUESDAY.
//  startDate alone writes ONE day. Adding endDate writes EVERY date between
//  them inclusive, each carrying the same set of periods.
//
//  THE YEAR IS IN THE PATH; THE DATES ARE IN THE BODY. Reversed 2026-09-17.
//  The year used to be DERIVED from the date, and a school holding two years
//  whose ranges both covered a September date had its timetable written into
//  the year it had NOT picked. Every date resolves to SOMETHING, so "you asked
//  for the wrong year" was not a sentence this endpoint could say. A date
//  outside the named year is now 409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR. The dates stay
//  in the body because #1 writes a RANGE.
//
//  A HOLIDAY IS SKIPPED; A TAKEN DATE REFUSES EVERYTHING.
//  A holiday inside a range is EXPECTED — any range longer than about five
//  days contains a weekly off — so it is skipped and NAMED in the response.
//  A date that already has a timetable is NOT expected: it means something is
//  being rebuilt, and half a range leaves a school unable to tell which days
//  came from which request.
//
//  A WEEKLY OFF IS A DATED HOLIDAY, NEVER A WEEKDAY. Nothing here looks at the
//  day of the week. A school that runs on Sunday is a normal school.
//
//  A LESSON MAY ONLY NAME A SUBJECT THAT SECTION ACTUALLY STUDIES.
//    - created WITHOUT a sectionNo -> class-wide, every section takes it
//    - created WITH a sectionNo    -> that section alone
//  Matching only the section's own subjects would refuse Maths for the whole
//  school; ignoring sectionNo would let 10-A be timetabled for German it does
//  not take, and nobody would notice until a child sat an exam in it.
//
//  TOUCHING PERIODS DO NOT CLASH. 09:00-09:45 beside 09:45-10:30 is a normal
//  day — the opposite call from a grade band, whose bounds are inclusive.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  ONE DAY                        -> 201  startDate only. createdCount 1,
//                                           and the whole day in `timetable`.
// 02  A RANGE                        -> 201  one timetable per date, and NO
//                                           `timetable` — a fortnight of a
//                                           400-period school is 4,000 entries
//                                           the caller already holds.
// 03  A HOLIDAY IN THE RANGE         -> 201  that date is in skippedDates with
//                                           its NAME; the rest are written.
// 04  EVERY DATE IS A HOLIDAY        -> 409  NOT_A_WORKING_DAY. Nothing written.
// 05  A DATE ALREADY HAS ONE         -> 409  TIMETABLE_ALREADY_EXISTS, naming
//                                           every colliding date. NOTHING is
//                                           written, not even the free dates.
// 06  A CLASS-WIDE SUBJECT           -> 201  Maths, in any section.
// 07  A SECTION SUBJECT IN ITS OWN   -> 201  German in 10-C.
// 08  THE SAME IN ANOTHER SECTION    -> 409  SUBJECT_NOT_IN_SECTION. The case
//                                           that makes the rule worth having.
// 09  A RETIRED SUBJECT              -> 409  SUBJECT_NOT_IN_SECTION. Unlike a
//                                           retired grading scheme, nothing is
//                                           being reprinted — the day has not
//                                           happened yet.
// 10  BACK-TO-BACK PERIODS           -> 201  touching is not overlapping.
// 11  ONE TEACHER, TWO SECTIONS      -> 409  TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP.
// 12  TWO SECTIONS, ONE ROOM         -> 409  ROOM_PERIOD_OVERLAP. Without a
//                                           room, two sections at one time is
//                                           the normal case.
// 13  A BREAK WITH A TEACHER         -> 201  ALLOWED since 2026-09-17 — somebody
//                                           supervises lunch, and it still
//                                           counts against their day. A
//                                           SUBJECT on one is still a 400.
// 14  AN UNLISTED SUNDAY             -> 201  a working day like any other.
// 15  A DATE IN ANOTHER YEAR         -> 409  DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR. THE
//                                           CASE THIS REWORK EXISTS FOR.
// 16  A YEAR THAT IS NOT RUNNING     -> 409  ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_RUNNING (gate 4).
// 16  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL             -> 409  SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE (gate 1).
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED           The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 INVALID_DATE_RANGE            endDate is before startDate.
//     400 DATE_RANGE_TOO_LONG           More than 120 days — about a term of working days.
//     400 INVALID_PERIOD_TIMES          startTime is not before endTime. Equal times too.
//     400 SLOT_FIELDS_REQUIRED          A LESSON with no subjectCode or teacherDocsId.
//     400 SLOT_FIELDS_NOT_ALLOWED       A non-lesson carrying a SUBJECT. A teacher is fine.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND              No school has that subdomain.
//     404 CLASS_NOT_FOUND               Not this school's class in that year.
//     404 TEACHER_NOT_FOUND             Not staff of this school.
//     409 TIMETABLE_ALREADY_EXISTS      A date in the range already has one.
//     409 NOT_A_WORKING_DAY             Every date is a holiday.
//     404 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_FOUND       No year of that name in this school.
//     409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR    A date falls outside the year named in the path.
//     409 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_RUNNING     That year is not the one the school is running.
//     409 SECTION_NOT_IN_CLASS          Not an active section of that class.
//     409 SUBJECT_NOT_IN_SECTION        That section does not study it.
//     409 SECTION_PERIOD_OVERLAP        One section in two places at once.
//     409 TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP        One teacher in two places at once.
//     409 ROOM_PERIOD_OVERLAP           Two sections in one room.
//     409 PERIOD_CODE_TAKEN             One section names a period code twice.
//     409 SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE             Gate 1.

body.raw:
// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  GET /academic-years/{year}/timetables
//
//  A LIST CARRIES WHAT A LIST NEEDS. A full school day measures about 120 KB,
//  so a page of twenty carrying its periods would be 2.5 MB shipped to render
//  twenty dates. The five counts are worked out by an AGGREGATION and the
//  entries array never leaves the database. #7 is one call away for a day in
//  full.
//
//  THE FIVE COUNTS
//     entryCount    every period of every section that day
//     lessonCount   how many are TAUGHT — the rest are breaks and assemblies
//     classCount    distinct classes with anything scheduled
//     sectionCount  distinct CLASS-AND-SECTION pairs — "A" of one class and
//                   "A" of another are two
//     teacherCount  distinct staff named anywhere, a break's supervisor too
//
//  NONE OF THEM IS SORTABLE. They are not on the document; the aggregation
//  computes them AFTER the page is chosen, so ordering by one would mean
//  counting the whole year first.
//
//  ?from= AND ?to= ARE INDEPENDENT. Either alone is meaningful, neither is
//  required, and a window with nothing in it is an EMPTY PAGE, never a 404.
//
//  ?classDocsId= WITH ?sectionNo= ARE MATCHED AS ONE PERIOD. A day holds every
//  class's periods, so two separate conditions would match a day where one
//  entry belongs to class X and an unrelated entry belongs to some other
//  class's section B — which is nearly every day. It is an $elemMatch.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  A YEAR'S DAYS                  -> 200  oldest first, five counts each,
//                                           and NO periods on any row.
// 02  A DATE WINDOW                  -> 200  ?from= and ?to=, together or
//                                           either one alone.
// 03  A WINDOW WITH NOTHING IN IT    -> 200  an empty page, never a 404.
// 04  ONE TEACHER'S WORKING DAYS     -> 200  ?teacherDocsId=. A day where they
//                                           only SUPERVISE a break counts.
// 05  ONE ROOM'S DAYS                -> 200  ?facilityResourceDocsId=.
// 06  A CLASS AND A SECTION, PAIRED  -> 200  a day holding class X section A
//                                           and class Y section B does NOT
//                                           match "X + B".
// 07  AN UNSUPERVISED BREAK          -> 200  teacherCount 1, not 2. The field
//                                           is ABSENT rather than null, and in
//                                           an aggregation $ne null would have
//                                           counted it — measured.
// 08  SORTING BY A COUNT             -> 400  INVALID_SORT_FIELD.
// 09  A YEAR THAT HAS ENDED          -> 200  no gate runs on a read.
// 10  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL             -> 200  likewise.
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED           The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 INVALID_SORT_FIELD            ?sort= named a field off the allowlist.
//     400 INVALID_PAGE_SIZE             ?size= above 100 or below 1.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND              No school has that subdomain.
//     404 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_FOUND       No year of that name in this school.

{
  "sourceDate": "{{timetableDate}}",
  "classDocsId": "",
  "sectionNo": "",
  "merge": false
}

request.description:
// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  POST /academic-years/{year}/timetables/{date}/copy-from
//
//  WHAT A SCHOOL ACTUALLY DOES. Nobody types five days: Monday is built once
//  and Tuesday through Friday are copied from it and then corrected. Without
//  this, a week of a 400-period school is 2,000 periods typed by hand — and
//  the typing is where the mistakes come from.
//
//  THE TARGET IS IN THE PATH; THE SOURCE IS IN THE BODY. The day being BUILT
//  is what this endpoint acts on, so it is the address — the same reading that
//  puts {date} in the path on #2 and #7.
//
//  NEW IDS FOR EVERY COPIED PERIOD, ALWAYS. They are different periods on a
//  different date. Two days sharing an entry id would make
//  AttendanceSession.timetableEntryId ambiguous, which is the single thing
//  generated ids exist to prevent — so a copy GENERATES rather than reuses,
//  even though it is copying. Everything else is carried across field for
//  field, in the order the source held it. The source itself is untouched.
//
//  THE TARGET IS VALIDATED THE WAY #1 BUILDS A DAY — its own year, its own
//  holiday check. COPYING MONDAY ONTO A FESTIVAL IS REFUSED.
//  That is where it differs from #1, which SKIPS a holiday: #1 takes a RANGE
//  and any range longer than about five days contains a weekly off, so
//  skipping is the only way ranges stay usable. A copy names ONE date, and a
//  caller who named a festival meant a different day.
//
//  THE SOURCE MUST BE IN THE SAME ACADEMIC YEAR. A class belongs to exactly
//  one, so last year's Monday names classDocsIds this year does not have.
//
//  A DAY CANNOT BE BUILT FROM ITSELF — 400 SOURCE_IS_TARGET. Without a merge
//  it is a no-op dressed as a write; with one it would duplicate every period
//  onto itself and every duplicate would clash with the original.
//
//  THE FILTERS COPY PART OF A DAY
//     classDocsId              one class
//     classDocsId + sectionNo  one section — WHAT A SCHOOL ADDING A SECTION
//                              MID-TERM WANTS
//     sectionNo alone          400 SECTION_WITHOUT_CLASS. "Section A" is not
//                              one thing across a school; the same pairing
//                              rule #10 applies with its $elemMatch.
//  A filter that matches NOTHING is 409 NOTHING_TO_COPY, not an empty success.
//
//  MERGING RE-RUNS EVERY CHECK AGAINST THE COMBINED LIST. Without merge, a
//  target that already has a timetable is 409 TIMETABLE_ALREADY_EXISTS. With
//  it, the copied periods are ADDED — and a teacher free in Monday and free in
//  Tuesday can be in two places once Monday's periods are added to Tuesday's.
//  So the overlap, period-code and structure checks all run on the combined
//  list, never on what arrived. Periods already in the target KEEP THEIR IDS.
//
//  NO version IS REQUIRED, unlike #2: a merge cannot erase a period the caller
//  never saw. The save still carries @Version, so a writer that got in between
//  is 409 CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION rather than a lost edit.
//
//  201 WHEN IT BUILT A DAY, 200 WHEN IT MERGED INTO ONE. The status says
//  whether something came into being.
//
//  ALL THREE GATES RUN.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  TUESDAY FROM MONDAY            -> 201  every period carried across, in
//                                           the source's order, with NEW ids.
//                                           copiedCount n, keptCount 0,
//                                           merged false.
// 02  THE IDS ARE NEW                -> 201  compare the target's entry ids
//                                           with the source's — NOT ONE IS
//                                           SHARED.
// 03  THE SOURCE IS UNTOUCHED        -> 201  read it back after copying.
// 04  ONE CLASS ONLY                 -> 201  + classDocsId.
// 05  ONE SECTION ONLY               -> 201  + classDocsId and sectionNo.
//                                           sectionNo folds case.
// 06  A SECTION WITH NO CLASS        -> 400  SECTION_WITHOUT_CLASS.
// 07  A FILTER MATCHING NOTHING      -> 409  NOTHING_TO_COPY, and NO day is
//                                           created.
// 08  A TARGET THAT ALREADY EXISTS   -> 409  TIMETABLE_ALREADY_EXISTS,
//                                           offering merge and #2.
// 09  MERGING INTO IT                -> 200  not 201 — nothing came into
//                                           being. keptCount keeps its ids.
// 10  A MERGE THAT CLASHES           -> 409  two days each valid ALONE whose
//                                           teacher is shared at one hour:
//                                           TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP. Nothing
//                                           is written. THE WHOLE RISK OF
//                                           MERGING.
// 11  ONTO A HOLIDAY                 -> 409  NOT_A_WORKING_DAY, NAMING it.
// 12  A DAY FROM ITSELF              -> 400  SOURCE_IS_TARGET.
// 13  A SOURCE THAT DOES NOT EXIST   -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND.
// 14  A SOURCE FROM ANOTHER YEAR     -> 409  DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR.
// 15  A YEAR THAT IS NOT RUNNING     -> 409  ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_RUNNING, gate 4.
// 16  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL             -> 409  SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE, gate 1.
// 17  ANOTHER SCHOOL'S SOURCE        -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND.
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED           The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 VALIDATION_FAILED             sourceDate is absent.
//     400 SOURCE_IS_TARGET              sourceDate is the date in the path.
//     400 SECTION_WITHOUT_CLASS         sectionNo with no classDocsId beside it.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND              No school has that subdomain.
//     404 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_FOUND       No year of that name in this school.
//     404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND           The source date has no timetable.
//     404 CLASS_NOT_FOUND               A copied period names a class this year lacks.
//     404 TEACHER_NOT_FOUND             A copied period names somebody no longer staff.
//     409 NOTHING_TO_COPY               The filter matched no period of the source.
//     409 TIMETABLE_ALREADY_EXISTS      The target has one and merge was not asked for.
//     409 NOT_A_WORKING_DAY             The target date is a holiday or weekly off.
//     409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR    The target is outside {year}, or the source is another year's.
//     409 SECTION_NOT_IN_CLASS          A copied section is no longer active.
//     409 SUBJECT_NOT_IN_SECTION        A copied subject is no longer that section's.
//     409 SECTION_PERIOD_OVERLAP        Merging puts one section in two places.
//     409 TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP        Merging puts one teacher in two places.
//     409 ROOM_PERIOD_OVERLAP           Merging sends two sections to one room.
//     409 PERIOD_CODE_TAKEN             Merging repeats a period code in a section.
//     409 CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION       Another write changed the target first.
//     409 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_RUNNING     Gate 4.
//     409 SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE             Gate 1.

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  PUT /academic-years/{year}/timetables/{date}
//
//  THE ONE FULL-DOCUMENT WRITE, AND THE ONE TO REACH FOR LAST. Every other
//  write in this module exists so that this one is not needed: #3 adds a
//  period, #4 corrects one (the substitution the module exists for), #5
//  removes one, #6 copies a day. THIS ONE OVERWRITES EVERYTHING, and a period
//  left out of the list is GONE. The module's own plan calls it "a footgun
//  with an audit trail". Three things blunt it.
//
//  1. version IS REQUIRED
//  A targeted update touches one embedded entry and cannot lose somebody
//  else's edit to another. A replace can lose all of them. So the caller says
//  which version it is replacing, and a day that moved on is
//  409 CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION naming BOTH versions.
//  It comes from #7, or from #1's echo — both now return it, because a
//  required field with no way to obtain it would be a refusal nobody could
//  satisfy. It is checked TWICE: once against the document just read (which
//  produces that message) and once by @Version on the save (which closes the
//  window between that read and the write).
//
//  2. ENTRY IDS ARE KEPT WHERE THEY ARE SENT
//  Send a period's timetableEntryId back and it KEEPS ITS IDENTITY, so an
//  AttendanceSession pointing at it still does. Leave it off — or send it
//  blank — for a period being added, and one is generated.
//  An id not in THIS day is 404 TIMETABLE_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND, INCLUDING A REAL ID
//  BELONGING TO ANOTHER DATE: one id on two days would make timetableEntryId
//  ambiguous, which is the single thing generated ids exist to prevent.
//
//  3. THE RESPONSE NAMES EVERY REMOVED ID
//  Not a count — the ids. This is the destructive half, and those ids are what
//  an attendance session may still point at. THE ENDPOINT DOES NOT REFUSE OVER
//  IT: open item 4 of the plan is unsettled and there is no attendance
//  repository yet. Visibility, not enforcement.
//
//  IT REPLACES; IT DOES NOT CREATE. A date with no timetable is
//  404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND pointing at #1. The pair keeps both honest: #1
//  refuses a taken date, #2 refuses a free one.
//
//  EVERY RULE #1 APPLIES, THIS APPLIES — the section normalised to the class's
//  own spelling, a subject that section actually studies, the three overlap
//  checks, the period-code rule, every teacher being this school's. Literally
//  the same code: the structure step and the teacher check moved into utils
//  when this endpoint needed exactly them.
//
//  A DATE THAT HAS SINCE BECOME A HOLIDAY CAN STILL BE CORRECTED, and that is
//  deliberate: #1 skips holidays because it CHOOSES its dates; this one is
//  handed a date that already has a document, and refusing would trap a school
//  that cannot delete the day either.
//
//  ALL THREE GATES RUN, unlike the reads. This is the most destructive write
//  in the module.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  KEEP EVERY PERIOD              -> 200  send every id back, change one
//                                           field. keptCount n, addedCount 0,
//                                           removedEntryIds []. Every id is
//                                           the one it came in with.
// 02  DROP A PERIOD                  -> 200  leave one out. Its id is in
//                                           removedEntryIds, BY NAME — the
//                                           closest thing to an undo.
// 03  ADD A PERIOD                   -> 200  one entry with NO id. addedCount
//                                           1, and a fresh id nobody had.
// 04  A BLANK ID IS "NEW"            -> 200  timetableEntryId: "" is the same
//                                           as leaving it off. A stored id is
//                                           never empty, so blank cannot be a
//                                           lookup.
// 05  A STALE VERSION                -> 409  CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION naming
//                                           BOTH versions. THE CASE THE WHOLE
//                                           ENDPOINT RESTS ON.
// 06  NO VERSION AT ALL              -> 400  required, unlike on every other
//                                           write here.
// 07  ANOTHER DAY'S REAL ENTRY ID    -> 404  TIMETABLE_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND. A
//                                           genuine id, of a genuine day, that
//                                           is not THIS day's.
// 08  ONE ID SENT TWICE              -> 400  DUPLICATE_TIMETABLE_ENTRY_ID.
// 09  A DATE WITH NO TIMETABLE       -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND, pointing at
//                                           #1. NOT an upsert.
// 10  AN EMPTY DAY                   -> 400  entries: []. There is no way to
//                                           empty a day here, deliberately.
// 11  "A" AND "a" AT ONE TIME        -> 409  SECTION_PERIOD_OVERLAP. The
//                                           section is normalised to the
//                                           class's spelling FIRST.
// 12  A DAY UNDER THE WRONG YEAR     -> 409  DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR. The
//                                           stored academicYear is the
//                                           authority.
// 13  A DATE THAT BECAME A HOLIDAY   -> 200  allowed, deliberately.
// 14  A YEAR THAT IS NOT RUNNING     -> 409  ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_RUNNING, gate 4.
//                                           #7 still reads the same day.
// 15  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL             -> 409  SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE, gate 1.
// 16  ANOTHER SCHOOL'S DATE          -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND.
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED           The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 VALIDATION_FAILED             version absent, or entries empty or over 4000.
//     400 DUPLICATE_TIMETABLE_ENTRY_ID  One timetableEntryId appears twice in the request.
//     400 INVALID_PERIOD_TIMES          startTime is not before endTime.
//     400 SLOT_FIELDS_REQUIRED          A LESSON with no subjectCode or teacherDocsId.
//     400 SLOT_FIELDS_NOT_ALLOWED       A non-lesson carrying a subject. A teacher is fine.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND              No school has that subdomain.
//     404 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_FOUND       No year of that name in this school.
//     404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND           That date has no timetable — create it with #1.
//     404 TIMETABLE_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND     A sent id is not in THIS day.
//     404 CLASS_NOT_FOUND               Not this school's class in that year.
//     404 TEACHER_NOT_FOUND             Not staff of this school.
//     409 CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION       The version sent is not the day's current version.
//     409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR    The stored day belongs to a different year.
//     409 SECTION_NOT_IN_CLASS          Not an active section of that class.
//     409 SUBJECT_NOT_IN_SECTION        That section does not study it.
//     409 SECTION_PERIOD_OVERLAP        One section in two places at once.
//     409 TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP        One teacher in two places at once.
//     409 ROOM_PERIOD_OVERLAP           Two sections in one room.
//     409 PERIOD_CODE_TAKEN             One section names a period code twice.
//     409 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_RUNNING     Gate 4.
//     409 SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE             Gate 1.

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  POST /…/timetables/{date}/entries
//
//  A $push, NEVER A RE-SAVE. A day is about 120 KB. Rewriting all of it to
//  add one period would make every addition a race with every other edit of
//  that morning, and would overwrite periods the caller never saw. Read the
//  day back with #7 afterwards: EVERY PERIOD THAT WAS THERE KEEPS ITS ID.
//
//  CHECKED AGAINST THE WHOLE DAY, NOT ON ITS OWN. Whether the new period fits
//  BESIDE the ones already there is the only thing worth checking, so the
//  overlap, slot and structure rules run over the combined list.
//
//  THE PERIOD CODE IS GUARDED IN THE UPDATE ITSELF — $push matched on "this
//  section has no period with this code". It is the one conflict rule
//  expressible WITHOUT COMPARING TIMES, and the one most likely to be raced,
//  because a period code is what a person types twice.
//
//  WHY ONLY THAT ONE: a stored LocalTime is a BSON date carrying THE DAY THE
//  DOCUMENT WAS WRITTEN — 09:00 became 2026-09-17T03:30:00Z — so two days
//  written a day apart cannot have their times compared in a query at all.
//  Overlap is checked in Java against the day just read, which leaves a narrow
//  race: two clerks adding OVERLAPPING periods with DIFFERENT codes in the
//  same instant would both be accepted. Open item 8 of the plan.
//
//  THE DAY'S VERSION MOVES. A targeted write that left it standing still would
//  let #2 replace a day that had gained a period.
//
//  THE DAY MUST ALREADY EXIST — 404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND. Creating one is #1.
//
//  ALL THREE GATES RUN.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  ONE PERIOD INTO A DAY        -> 201  with a generated timetableEntryId.
// 02  THE DAY'S VERSION MOVES      -> 201  compare #7 before and after.
// 03  A REPEATED PERIOD CODE       -> 409  PERIOD_CODE_TAKEN.
// 04  THE SAME CODE ELSEWHERE      -> 201  codes are per SECTION.
// 05  OVERLAPPING ITS OWN SECTION  -> 409  SECTION_PERIOD_OVERLAP.
// 06  A TEACHER ALREADY TEACHING   -> 409  TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP.
// 07  A ROOM ALREADY IN USE        -> 409  ROOM_PERIOD_OVERLAP.
// 08  A LOWER-CASE SECTION         -> 201  stored in the class's spelling.
// 09  A LESSON WITH NO TEACHER     -> 400  SLOT_FIELDS_REQUIRED.
// 10  A BREAK WITH A SUBJECT       -> 400  SLOT_FIELDS_NOT_ALLOWED.
// 11  A DAY WITH NO TIMETABLE      -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND.
// 12  A HOLIDAY                    -> 404  NOT_A_WORKING_DAY.
// 13  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL           -> 409  SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE, gate 1.

