// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  PATCH /…/timetables/{date}/entries/{entryId}
//
//  THE WRITE THIS MODULE EXISTS FOR. A teacher calls in sick at 07:40 and six
//  periods need covering before 08:00. Each is one field of one period, so
//  this is one targeted $set entries.$[entry].<field> through an array filter
//  — not a re-save of 120 KB, and not a replacement of the day.
//
//  EDITABLE: teacherDocsId, subjectCode, startTime, endTime, slotLabel,
//            facilityResourceDocsId, periodCode.
//  NOT EDITABLE: classDocsId and sectionNo — moving a period to another
//            section is deleting one and adding another, and pretending
//            otherwise keeps an attendance session pointing at a period that
//            changed identity underneath it. Nor slotType, which decides which
//            other fields are legal: a LESSON turned into a BREAK in place
//            would leave a subject on a break.
//  Sending them is not an error; they are simply not in the request shape.
//
//  "" CLEARS; AN ABSENT KEY LEAVES THE FIELD ALONE. A room is removed by
//  sending facilityResourceDocsId: "", and there is no other way to say it.
//  It does not apply to periodCode, startTime and endTime, which a period
//  cannot be without. A patch that changes nothing is 400 NOTHING_TO_UPDATE.
//
//  EXACTLY ONE DOCUMENT MUST MATCH — AND MATCHED IS NOT MODIFIED.
//  Zero matched means the entry is gone or the version moved: 404 or 409,
//  never a silent success. ZERO MODIFIED MEANS NOTHING OF THE SORT — a $set
//  writing the value a field already holds changes nothing and is a perfectly
//  good no-op. PATCHING A TEACHER TO THE TEACHER ALREADY THERE IS A 200.
//
//  THE CORRECTED PERIOD IS CHECKED BESIDE THE OTHERS, which is the whole point
//  of a substitution: THE COVERING TEACHER MUST NOT ALREADY BE SOMEWHERE ELSE
//  AT THAT HOUR.
//
//  version IS OPTIONAL HERE, unlike #2. A targeted write cannot lose somebody
//  else's edit to a DIFFERENT period, so requiring it would refuse two clerks
//  working on two sections — open item 1's complaint. Send it when the
//  correction was decided from a screen that might be stale.
//
//  ALL THREE GATES RUN.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  THE SUBSTITUTION             -> 200  teacherDocsId and nothing else.
//                                         Read #7 back: NOTHING ELSE MOVED.
// 02  A NO-OP PATCH                -> 200  send the teacher it already has.
//                                         NOT 404. THE TRAP.
// 03  NOTHING AT ALL               -> 400  NOTHING_TO_UPDATE.
// 04  CLEARING THE ROOM            -> 200  "" makes the field ABSENT.
// 05  CLEARING A LESSON'S TEACHER  -> 400  SLOT_FIELDS_REQUIRED.
// 06  A TEACHER ALREADY BUSY       -> 409  TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP.
// 07  TIMES THAT NOW COLLIDE       -> 409  SECTION_PERIOD_OVERLAP.
// 08  A CODE THE SECTION USES      -> 409  PERIOD_CODE_TAKEN.
// 09  A STALE VERSION              -> 409  CONCURRENT_MODIFICATION, naming
//                                         both.
// 10  NO VERSION AT ALL            -> 200  optional here.
// 11  CLASS, SECTION, SLOT TYPE    -> 200  sent alongside a real change and
//                                         ignored.
// 12  A MALFORMED ENTRY ID         -> 404  TIMETABLE_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND, never a
//                                         500 — the presence check runs
//                                         before anything parses an ObjectId.
// 13  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL           -> 409  SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE, gate 1.

body.raw:
// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  DELETE /…/timetables/{date}/entries/{entryId}
//
//  A $pull BY ID. One period leaves; every other keeps its id and its place.
//  The day's VERSION STILL MOVES, so #2 can tell that it changed.
//
//  A 204, AND A 404 WHEN IT WAS NOT THERE. NOT an idempotent 204 either way: a
//  caller deleting a period that has already gone has a stale screen, and
//  telling them it worked would leave them believing they removed something
//  somebody else had already dealt with. HERE THE MODIFIED COUNT IS THE RIGHT
//  SIGNAL, unlike #4: a $pull that removes nothing modifies nothing.
//
//  REFUSED WHEN ATTENDANCE NAMES THE PERIOD — 409 ENTRY_STILL_REFERENCED.
//  AttendanceSession.timetableEntryId is an optional link with no foreign key
//  behind it, so the removal would leave any session naming that period
//  pointing at nothing and NOTHING WOULD FAIL. Open item 4 proposed refusing
//  instead, and that is what this does — one query per removal, SCOPED BY
//  SCHOOL so another tenant's session cannot block a removal this school is
//  entitled to make.
//  NOTHING WRITES THAT COLLECTION YET, so the refusal cannot fire through the
//  API today. Reaching it needs a session inserted directly.
//
//  NO version. A $pull by id is position-independent and cannot lose a
//  concurrent edit to another period — open item 1's table says so.
//
//  ALL THREE GATES RUN.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  REMOVE ONE PERIOD            -> 204  read #7 back: it is gone, every
//                                         other period KEPT ITS ID, and the
//                                         version moved.
// 02  REMOVE IT AGAIN              -> 404  TIMETABLE_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND. NOT a
//                                         second 204.
// 03  A MALFORMED ENTRY ID         -> 404  the same, never a 500.
// 04  A PERIOD ATTENDANCE NAMES    -> 409  ENTRY_STILL_REFERENCED, and the
//                                         period is STILL THERE.
// 05  ANOTHER SCHOOL'S SESSION     -> 204  does not block it.
// 06  A DAY WITH NO TIMETABLE      -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND.
// 07  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL           -> 409  SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE, gate 1.

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  GET /…/timetables/{date}/sections/{classDocsId}/{sectionNo}
//
//  WHAT A CHILD'S PARENT OPENS. Nobody outside the office wants the whole
//  school's four hundred periods; they want the eight their child sits
//  through.
//
//  EARLIEST FIRST, WHERE #7 IS IN STORED ORDER. #7 returns the whole school's
//  day as stored and says why: periods of different SECTIONS run at the same
//  hour, so "by time" there is not an order at all — it is a tie with a hidden
//  second key. THAT OBJECTION DOES NOT APPLY TO ONE SECTION: a section cannot
//  be in two places at once (SECTION_PERIOD_OVERLAP is refused on every
//  write), so within one section startTime is a TOTAL order. Sorting here is
//  meaningful where sorting there would have been a guess.
//
//  AN EMPTY ANSWER IS A 200. The date has a timetable and this section has
//  nothing in it: a fact about the section, not a missing document. The three
//  404s belong to the DAY, and they are the same three #7 gives.
//
//  "NOTHING SCHEDULED" AND "WRONG SECTION" NEVER LOOK ALIKE. A mistyped
//  classDocsId is 404 CLASS_NOT_FOUND; a section the class does not hold is
//  409 SECTION_NOT_IN_CLASS. Returning an empty list for either would leave a
//  parent's app unable to tell "no school today" from "I asked for the wrong
//  child".
//
//  A RETIRED SECTION STILL ANSWERS, and this does NOT use the active-section
//  check every write uses. No gate runs on a read: a section retired in March
//  must not make February's Tuesday unreadable, because attendance taken
//  against it has to stay explicable.
//
//  THE NAMES COME RESOLVED — className, subjectName, teacherName — and the
//  subject name follows the same class-wide rule #1 enforces, so two sections
//  under one subjectCode get two different names.
//
//  FILTERED IN MEMORY, NOT WITH A PROJECTION. The document is already loaded,
//  and a $elemMatch projection returns only the FIRST match — exactly wrong
//  for a read whose job is every period of one section.
//
//  NO GATE. Last year's Tuesday still answers.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  A SECTION'S DAY               -> 200  only that section's periods,
//                                          EARLIEST FIRST.
// 02  NOT AS STORED                 -> 200  compare with #7 for the same
//                                          date: #7 gives the whole school in
//                                          the order it was written.
// 03  THE SECTION FOLDS CASE        -> 200  ask for 'a'; sectionNo answers as
//                                          'A', the class's own spelling.
// 04  ONE CODE, TWO SECTIONS        -> 200  a subjectCode used twice in one
//                                          class with a different sectionNo
//                                          resolves to a DIFFERENT
//                                          subjectName per section.
// 05  A SECTION WITH NOTHING ON     -> 200  entryCount 0 and an empty list.
//                                          NOT a 404.
// 06  A SECTION THE CLASS LACKS     -> 409  SECTION_NOT_IN_CLASS. This is why
//                                          05 is a 200.
// 07  A CLASS FROM ANOTHER YEAR     -> 404  CLASS_NOT_FOUND.
// 08  A HOLIDAY                     -> 404  NOT_A_WORKING_DAY, naming it.
// 09  A DAY WITH NO TIMETABLE       -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND.
// 10  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL            -> 200  no gate runs on a read.
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED           The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 BAD_REQUEST                   {date} is not an ISO date.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND              No school has that subdomain.
//     404 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_FOUND       No year of that name in this school.
//     404 CLASS_NOT_FOUND               Not this school's class in that year.
//     404 NOT_A_WORKING_DAY             That date is a holiday or weekly off.
//     404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND           A working day with nothing written yet.
//     409 SECTION_NOT_IN_CLASS          The class has no section of that name.
//     409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR    The stored day belongs to another year.

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  GET /…/timetables/{date}/teachers/{teacherDocsId}
//
//  WHAT A TEACHER'S APP OPENS. One person, one date, in the order their day
//  happens.
//
//  A BREAK THEY SUPERVISE IS PART OF THEIR DAY. Since 2026-09-17 a non-lesson
//  may carry a teacherDocsId — somebody supervises lunch, runs the assembly,
//  takes the activity. Those periods are IN this list, and they are why
//  lessonCount and entryCount differ: A TEACHER WITH NO LESSONS CAN STILL HAVE
//  A WORKING DAY, and a teacher named on a break cannot also be teaching
//  period 4.
//
//  EARLIEST FIRST, for the reason #8 is: a teacher cannot be in two places at
//  once (TEACHER_PERIOD_OVERLAP is refused on every write), so startTime is a
//  TOTAL order within one person's day. #7 returns the whole school in stored
//  order because there it is not.
//
//  AN UNKNOWN ID IS A 404, NOT A FREE DAY. An app that could not tell those
//  apart would show an empty morning to somebody whose id it had got wrong.
//  A REAL person with nothing that day is a 200 with an empty list and NO
//  firstStartTime or lastEndTime — absent rather than null, because there is
//  no first period to report a time for.
//
//  IT IS NOT "WHO IS FREE". firstStartTime and lastEndTime bracket what this
//  person is COMMITTED TO; the gaps between are not computed here, and #12 is
//  the endpoint that answers coverage — against every member of staff rather
//  than one — and it is not built.
//
//  sectionCount PAIRS CLASS WITH SECTION, because "A" of one class and "A" of
//  another are two.
//
//  STAFF ARE NOT YEAR-SCOPED, so this endpoint reaches the day check where #8
//  stops at the class: a day asked for under a year it does not belong to is
//  409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR here and 404 CLASS_NOT_FOUND there.
//
//  NO GATE. Last year's Tuesday still answers.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  A TEACHER'S DAY               -> 200  their periods, EARLIEST FIRST,
//                                          each naming the class and section.
// 02  A BREAK THEY SUPERVISE        -> 200  it is IN the list. Somebody who
//                                          ONLY supervises a break has
//                                          entryCount 1 and lessonCount 0.
// 03  AN UNSUPERVISED BREAK         -> 200  belongs to nobody's day.
// 04  THE ENDS OF THE DAY           -> 200  the LAST PERIOD'S END, not the
//                                          last start.
// 05  SECTIONS ARE PAIRED           -> 200  sectionCount pairs class WITH
//                                          section.
// 06  A FREE DAY                    -> 200  entryCount 0, an empty list, and
//                                          NO firstStartTime or lastEndTime.
// 07  AN UNKNOWN ID                 -> 404  TEACHER_NOT_FOUND. This is why 06
//                                          is a 200.
// 08  A HOLIDAY                     -> 404  NOT_A_WORKING_DAY, naming it.
// 09  A DAY UNDER THE WRONG YEAR    -> 409  DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR.
// 10  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL            -> 200  no gate runs on a read.
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED           The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 BAD_REQUEST                   {date} is not an ISO date.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND              No school has that subdomain.
//     404 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_FOUND       No year of that name in this school.
//     404 TEACHER_NOT_FOUND             That id is not staff of this school.
//     404 NOT_A_WORKING_DAY             That date is a holiday or weekly off.
//     404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND           A working day with nothing written yet.
//     409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR    The stored day belongs to another year.

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  GET /academic-years/{year}/timetables/{date}
//
//  ADDRESSED BY THE DATE, NOT BY A DOCUMENT ID. Every other detail read in this
//  collection takes a MongoDB id. This one takes an ISO date, for one reason:
//  A CALLER ALWAYS KNOWS THE DATE AND NEVER KNOWS THE ID. A teacher's app asks
//  what is on today; nothing asks what is on in document 67aa15...
//  school_timetable_date_uniq, unique on schoolId + date, makes the date enough.
//
//  THE NAMES COME BACK WITH THE IDS
//     className      what the school calls the class
//     subjectName    resolved by the SAME rule #1 enforces: a subject with no
//                    sectionNo is class-wide, one with a sectionNo belongs to
//                    that section alone. Two sections under one subjectCode
//                    therefore get two DIFFERENT names.
//     teacherName    the staff member's fullName
//  Two extra queries, against about seventy round trips for a day of four
//  hundred periods across twelve classes and sixty staff.
//
//  A NAME THAT CANNOT BE FOUND IS LEFT OUT AND THE ID STAYS. A class deleted or
//  a staff member removed AFTER the day was written must not stop last Tuesday
//  from answering.
//
//  facilityResourceDocsId IS NOT RESOLVED. #1 never checks that a room exists
//  when it writes one, so a stored id may name nothing and a blank name would
//  hide that. Open item 3 of the module plan.
//
//  ENTRIES COME BACK IN STORED ORDER, NEVER RE-SORTED. Sorting by time is the
//  obvious choice and the wrong one: periods of different SECTIONS run at the
//  same hour, so "by time" is a tie with a hidden second key. Which grouping a
//  screen wants is the screen's question — #8, #9 and #11 answer it for one
//  section, one teacher and one room.
//
//  THE STORED YEAR DECIDES, NOT TODAY'S RANGE — measured 2026-09-17.
//  The day is read FIRST and what it says about itself is what answers.
//  Checking the date against the year's range BEFORE reading looked tidier and
//  was measured wrong: a school that later SHRINKS its year past an
//  already-written day made this endpoint answer 409 for a date #10 was still
//  listing. Two reads of one module disagreeing about whether a day exists is
//  worse than either answer. The range now only ever explains an ABSENCE.
//
//  THREE REFUSALS, AND THEY SAY THREE DIFFERENT THINGS
//     409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR  the caller's year and date disagree.
//                                     A mistake in the QUESTION.
//     404 NOT_A_WORKING_DAY           the school was closed, and the message
//                                     NAMES the holiday. Nothing is missing.
//     404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND         a working day with nothing written.
//                                     THIS is the one a school acts on.
//  A screen that could not tell the last two apart would be reporting a gap on
//  Independence Day.
//
//  THE SAME FIVE COUNTS AS A ROW OF #10, repeated rather than assumed to be in
//  hand: a caller reaching a day by a LINK never saw the list. They cost nothing
//  here, because the entries are already loaded.
//
//  NO GATE RUNS ON A READ. A suspended school still reads its own timetable, and
//  last year's Tuesday still answers — attendance taken against it has to stay
//  explicable.
// =========================================================================
//
// 01  A DAY THAT EXISTS             -> 200  every period of every section, in
//                                          stored order.
// 02  THE NAMES ARE RESOLVED        -> 200  className, subjectName, teacherName
//                                          beside the ids. Compare with #1's
//                                          echo, which carries ids only.
// 03  ONE CODE, TWO SECTIONS        -> 200  a subjectCode used twice in one
//                                          class with a different sectionNo
//                                          resolves to a DIFFERENT name per
//                                          section. The case that tells the
//                                          class-wide rule from "match by code".
// 04  A BREAK NOBODY SUPERVISES     -> 200  teacherDocsId and teacherName both
//                                          ABSENT, not null. A break WITH a
//                                          supervisor carries both — allowed
//                                          since 2026-09-17.
// 05  A ROOM IS NOT RESOLVED        -> 200  facilityResourceDocsId is an id and
//                                          there is no room name.
// 06  A HOLIDAY                     -> 404  NOT_A_WORKING_DAY, naming it.
// 07  A WORKING DAY, NOTHING ON IT  -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND. The one to act
//                                          on, and why 06 is a separate code.
// 08  A DATE OUTSIDE THE YEAR       -> 409  DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR, naming
//                                          the year's own range.
// 09  A DAY UNDER THE WRONG YEAR    -> 409  the stored academicYear is the
//                                          authority. Reachable by moving a
//                                          year's dates onto a foreign day.
// 10  A YEAR WHOSE DATES MOVED      -> 200  a day written while the year covered
//                                          it STILL reads back after the year is
//                                          shrunk past it — and #7 agrees with
//                                          #10 about whether it exists.
// 11  A MALFORMED DATE              -> 400  ISO only. /timetables/02-11-2026.
// 12  A YEAR THAT HAS ENDED         -> 200  no gate runs on a read.
// 13  A SUSPENDED SCHOOL            -> 200  likewise.
// 14  ANOTHER SCHOOL'S DATE         -> 404  TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND. A real date of
//                                          a real school is still not yours.
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED           The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     400 BAD_REQUEST                   {date} is not an ISO date.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND              No school has that subdomain.
//     404 ACADEMIC_YEAR_NOT_FOUND       No year of that name in this school.
//     404 NOT_A_WORKING_DAY             That date is a holiday or weekly off.
//     404 TIMETABLE_NOT_FOUND           A working day with nothing written yet.
//     409 DATE_OUTSIDE_ACADEMIC_YEAR    Outside {year}, or the day belongs to another year.

// =========================================================================
//  TEST CASES  —  POST /schools/current/staff
// Needs X-School-Subdomain. fullName, dateOfBirth, gender, phoneNumber and
// emailAddress are ALL required as of 2026-09-15.
//
//  THE WRITE FIVE OTHER MODULES ARE WAITING FOR. Payroll, leave, reviews,
//  development and every teacher picker need a staffDocsId, and nothing else
//  in this product produces one.
//
//  employeeNo IS GENERATED, NEVER SENT. NumberSequenceService, atomically and
//  per school — so two simultaneous creates cannot share a number, and two
//  schools both hold EMP/2026/09/000001 without colliding. Sending one is
//  IGNORED, never refused.
//
//  THE SHAPE IS {PREFIX}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{000001}, the house style for every
//  generated number in this product. A school ALREADY NUMBERING KEEPS ITS
//  SHAPE: the stored template wins, because a number somebody has written
//  down must not change under them.
//
//  A PHONE AND AN EMAIL EACH IDENTIFY ONE PERSON within a school. Duplicates
//  are 409 STAFF_PHONE_TAKEN and 409 STAFF_EMAIL_TAKEN, checked AFTER
//  normalising — "+91 98765-43210" collides with "+919876543210". Both
//  indexes are PARTIAL, so any number of people may have neither.
//
//  FIVE REQUIRED FIELDS, AND THE PLAN SAID ONE. dateOfBirth and gender
//  because the MODEL declares them @NotNull; phoneNumber and emailAddress
//  because the SCHOOL asked for them — a staff record with no way to contact
//  the person is one the office has to chase later.
//
//  SO ?hasEmail=false ON #7 CAN NO LONGER MATCH ANYBODY CREATED HERE. It
//  still matches the rows written before this rule, which is who it is for.
//
//  "---" IS REFUSED with 400 STAFF_PHONE_REQUIRED: it passes the blank check
//  and then normalises to nothing.
//
//  THIS CREATES A PERSON, NOT AN EMPLOYEE. No status, no department, no
//  designation, no joining date — none is on the document. #16 writes the
//  job, and somebody created here and not yet hired is a REAL state.
//
//  NO COUNTRY CODE IS INVENTED. A number without a + is stored as given: a
//  wrong guess writes something that looks right and cannot be called.
// =========================================================================
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 01  A NAME, A BIRTHDAY AND A GENDER                     -> 201 Created
//     The smallest legal body. OUT: employeeNo EMP/{year}/000001 and a
//     nextStep saying this person is not employed yet.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 02  THE NUMBER INCREMENTS                               -> 201 Created
//     Send 01 twice. OUT: 000001 then 000002.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 03  A SECOND SCHOOL STARTS AT 1 AGAIN                   -> 201 Created
//     Same body, a different X-School-Subdomain. OUT: 000001 again — the
//     sequence is per school, which is why a caller may not supply one.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 04  SENDING AN employeeNo                               -> 201 Created
//     { ..., "employeeNo": "EMP/1900/000999" }
//     IGNORED, not refused. OUT: the generated number.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 05  A NAME ALONE                                        -> 400 Bad Request
//     { "fullName": "No Birthday" }
//     What the PLAN said should work. OUT: fieldErrors naming dateOfBirth,
//     gender, phoneNumber AND emailAddress.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 05b A PHONE WITH NO DIGITS                             -> 400 Bad Request
//     { ..., "phoneNumber": "---" }
//     OUT: { "code": "STAFF_PHONE_REQUIRED" }
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 06  BORN TOMORROW                                       -> 400 Bad Request
//     { ..., "dateOfBirth": "2999-01-01" } — @Past. A person born in the
//     future is a typo, and one stored is a payroll problem later.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 07  A GENDER OUTSIDE THE ENUM                           -> 400 Bad Request
//     MALE, FEMALE, OTHER — the shared Gender, not a people-specific one.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 08  JOB FIELDS IN THE BODY                              -> 201 Created
//     { ..., "active": false, "status": "ACTIVE", "departmentDocsId": "x",
//       "joiningDate": "2020-01-01" }
//     EVERY ONE IGNORED — none is on the document. OUT: no status, no
//     department, no joining date. A person is not employed by existing.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 09  AN EMAIL PASTED WITH WHITESPACE                     -> 201 Created
//     { ..., "emailAddress": "  Anita.Sharma@Example.COM  " }
//     OUT: "anita.sharma@example.com". Trimmed in the request record so
//     @Email judges the value that would be stored, then lower-cased.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 10  A DUPLICATE EMAIL                                   -> 409 Conflict
//     Send 09 twice. OUT: { "code": "STAFF_EMAIL_TAKEN" }. Send it in a
//     DIFFERENT CASE and it still collides. Another SCHOOL may hold it.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 10b A DUPLICATE PHONE                                   -> 409 Conflict
//     OUT: { "code": "STAFF_PHONE_TAKEN" }. Retype the same number with
//     different spacing and it still collides — the check runs AFTER
//     stripping. Many people with NO phone are always fine: the index is
//     partial, which is what stops a school holding exactly one of them.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 11  PHONE NUMBERS                                       -> 201 Created
//     "+91 98765-43210" -> "+919876543210"
//     "(022) 2345.6789"  -> "02223456789"
//     "9876543210"       -> "9876543210"  (AS GIVEN — nothing invented)
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 12  AN ALL-BLANK ADDRESS                                -> 201 Created
//     { ..., "currentAddress": { "city": "   ", "countryCode": "" } }
//     OUT: no currentAddress key — an empty address is no address, not an
//     object of six nulls.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 13  A PARTLY FILLED ADDRESS                             -> 201 Created
//     { ..., "currentAddress": { "city": "Pune", "countryCode": "in" } }
//     KEPT, and the country upper-cased. A city and nothing else is real.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 14  EVERYTHING AT ONCE                                  -> 201 Created
//     Both addresses and the emergency contact. OUT: the contact's phone
//     normalised the same way the person's own is.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  EVERY REFUSAL
//     400 VALIDATION_FAILED                fullName blank; dateOfBirth absent or future;
//                                          gender absent or outside the enum; malformed email.
//     400 TENANT_NOT_RESOLVED              The X-School-Subdomain header is missing or blank.
//     403 SCHOOL_NOT_ACTIVE                Gate 1 — suspended or closed.
//     404 SCHOOL_NOT_FOUND                 No school has that subdomain.
//     409 SUBSCRIPTION_NOT_USABLE          Gate 2 — the school is not paying.
//     409 STAFF_PHONE_TAKEN                That number is already somebody's, in this school.
//     409 STAFF_EMAIL_TAKEN                That address is already somebody's, in this school.

{
    "fullName": "Anita Sharma",
    "dateOfBirth": "1990-08-14",
    "gender": "FEMALE",
    "phoneNumber": "+919876543210",
    "emailAddress": "anita.sharma@example.com"
}

